---
number: 108
title: My Lord Has Garments
common_title: Out of the Ivory Palaces
author: Henry Barraclough
composer: Henry Barraclough
sp_category: "9"
topic: Christ’s Suffering and Death
notes: "AUTO: chorus detected heuristically — verify"
---

[verse 1]
My Lord has garments so wondrous fine,
And myrrh their texture fills;
Its fragrance reached to this heart of mine,
With joy my being thrills.

[chorus]
Out of the ivory palaces
Into the world of woe,
Only His great eternal love
Made my Saviour go.

[verse 2]
His life had also its sorrows sore,
For aloes had a part;
And when I think of the cross He bore,
My eyes with teardrops start.

[chorus]
Out of the ivory palaces
Into the world of woe,
Only His great eternal love
Made my Saviour go.

[verse 3]
His garments too were in cassia dipped,
With healing in a touch;
Each time my feet in some sin have slipped,
He took me from its clutch.

[chorus]
Out of the ivory palaces
Into the world of woe,
Only His great eternal love
Made my Saviour go.

[verse 4]
In garments glorious He will come
To open wide the door;
And I shall enter my heavenly home,
To dwell forever more.

[chorus]
Out of the ivory palaces
Into the world of woe,
Only His great eternal love
Made my Saviour go.
